#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428020} :activity/id #uuid "be1bd01d-d4e3-5a46-9543-53b198bb6268" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050726 :topic/id #uuid "61838998-9541-5ba0-829a-1421ece4cbfb" :topic/math-academy-id 3132} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428016 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b340a6b9-2150-5d09-b212-4169a2e45650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371362} {:answer/id #uuid "ed71c6ee-fc7e-5366-b928-3a0e8ac31c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371363} {:answer/id #uuid "3decd7ef-d6cb-5d2c-a821-182982c2d6d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371364} {:answer/id #uuid "f1659e51-bced-5253-9862-0e84267f1efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371365} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7eb61e-6225-5628-bd68-d73407f01588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371366}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371365} :answer-field/id #uuid "64e997c7-7524-5cf1-946e-c03af754bbbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9e6bed9f-9e8e-54a8-ba26-f0e8954ce7e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-96303" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=6$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0c463439-a38b-593e-b012-0bd4d757c8b7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428019}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428017 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e43e58b-e171-5bde-8e66-fa1047e3a5e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404838} {:answer/id #uuid "ad42d289-8707-56c1-bf83-45db2fe36ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404839} {:answer/id #uuid "999d413b-60b1-5bd7-abe7-54bb8a4e91b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404840} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe630aa-18c2-5c17-a5c7-5d9c6cf67fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404841} {:answer/id #uuid "7758da6c-c911-54cb-96cc-7117ea4fabca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404842}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404839} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca27652a-1905-5111-863a-956b813d6f8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "069d96cb-6a82-58f7-9653-ba67b55165b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-95912" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=4$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=16,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-16I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-12 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-16I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "42130988-8a9c-5c98-b57a-c5c217558433" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428018}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428018 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428015 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "089ec07a-5e86-5e2c-9ee0-7a59f0e9ce6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186428009} {:answer/id #uuid "7011776a-f74f-5038-88b7-bd75d4a9eff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186428010} {:answer/id #uuid "7450dd95-1930-5564-96d4-6c9e274f1579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186428011} {:answer/id #uuid "a9766852-3203-5031-bf4e-845ae1f644cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186428012} {:answer/id #uuid "da310c2d-2eb6-5830-855e-93aa19083cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186428013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428010} :answer-field/id #uuid "01f21526-a5f6-5403-8d94-60d82378f21e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186428014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "39a1a8bd-f69f-5f36-99bc-5bd95f9b782d" :question/math-academy-id "q-96238" :question/problem "$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}73 & 24 \\\\ 24 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=9.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=81.$ Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-81I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-81I=[\\begin{aligned}73 & 24 \\\\ 24 & 9\\end{aligned}]-81[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 24 \\\\ 24 & -72\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-81I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}|=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b772e15d-f5b9-5724-9449-b771545c21bc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428016}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428019 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404891 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4eaa68b-520c-58b4-a17b-57dd48766c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404893} {:answer/id #uuid "dad029c8-f1bd-5774-bd9b-6cc44666c74f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404894} {:answer/id #uuid "b1179464-c161-5f17-9d1b-ec79fe124e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404895} {:answer/id #uuid "13acfcf7-36f6-5edb-88df-5d728cfbb0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404896} {:answer/id #uuid "717ea9f0-7aa4-55d1-823a-4458379130c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404897}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404896} :answer-field/id #uuid "e29ceeea-5603-57fa-99fc-57e0cab72178" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5095a12c-e6f9-56a4-8ce5-1c1e4698b3dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-96360" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}65 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 80\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=8.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=81,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-81I & =[\\begin{aligned}65 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 80\\end{aligned}]-81[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-81I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=64,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-64I & =[\\begin{aligned}65 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 80\\end{aligned}]-64[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-64I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{32}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bc5679a7-ffcd-561e-8c7e-fd7ddec627e4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428020 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371283 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e784017-9f26-57fb-8d09-e2c85b00c13e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371285} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fd7571-0b27-57c2-bd8b-9ae270401cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371286} {:answer/id #uuid "053e86da-1624-5e0f-a032-984baf156135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371287} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7c0920-0814-53cc-a6b5-0e0aded26986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371288} {:answer/id #uuid "52d8a537-0c37-5e79-800b-5a598b1c9e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371289}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371287} :answer-field/id #uuid "06d08e03-206a-52a5-b561-b040a6082be8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7f6a8969-0c0e-547e-babb-6ff2c612e774" :question/math-academy-id "q-95903" :question/problem "$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}68 & 26 \\\\ 26 & 29\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ So, the second column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=16.$ Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-16I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-16I=[\\begin{aligned}68 & 26 \\\\ 26 & 29\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}52 & 26 \\\\ 26 & 13\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-16I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|=|-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}|+|\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}|=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eb2350bf-19b6-5869-b8de-89f0f6ea18f9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428017}}] :activity/title "Singular Value Decomposition of 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186428008}]}
